# Architecture for ArchiPy Apps

Use this when building applications that depend on the `archipy` package (not when maintaining ArchiPy itself).

Live concepts: https://syntaxarc.github.io/ArchiPy/getting-started/concepts/

## Layer Map

```text
your_app/
├── configs/          # BaseConfig subclasses, DI containers
├── models/           # Entities, DTOs, errors, types — data structures only
│   └── dtos/{domain}/
│       ├── domain/v{n}/   # Versioned — cross service boundary
│       └── repository/    # Internal — never versioned
├── repositories/
│   └── {domain}/
│       ├── adapters/              # Domain wrappers around ArchiPy adapters
│       └── {domain}_repository.py # Orchestrate adapters; map to DTOs
├── logics/
│   └── {domain}/         # Use-case / unit-of-work boundaries
└── services/
    └── {domain}/v{n}/    # Transport (HTTP/gRPC routers), versioned
```

Optional app-local helpers (prefer `archipy.helpers` first): `helpers/{utils,decorators,interceptors}/`.

## Call Flow

```text
services → logics (@atomic) → repositories → adapters → ArchiPy
```

## Layer Rules

### `models/`
- Data structures only — no business logic, no I/O.
- Prefer ArchiPy base entities/errors where applicable; subclass domain errors from ArchiPy `BaseError` hierarchy.
- DTOs: Pydantic `BaseModel`.
  - Public: `dtos/{domain}/domain/v{n}/` (versioned).
  - Internal: `dtos/{domain}/repository/` (never versioned).
- Naming detail lives in the models rule when editing `models/**`.

### `repositories/`
- Each domain owns its data access under `repositories/{domain}/`.
- `adapters/` — domain-specific wrappers around ArchiPy base adapters; entity construction and query building.
- `{domain}_repository.py` — orchestrates adapters, cache-aside, maps results to response DTOs.
- Prefer ArchiPy adapters via extras (`uv add "archipy[redis]"`) before inventing clients.
- Catch infrastructure exceptions at the boundary and re-raise as domain errors with `raise ... from e`.
- Never import or call repositories from a different domain.

### `logics/`
- Pure business rules: domain DTO in → domain DTO out; framework-agnostic (no FastAPI/gRPC imports).
- Unit of work: public methods use `@postgres_sqlalchemy_atomic_decorator` (or `async_postgres_sqlalchemy_atomic_decorator`) when Postgres SQLAlchemy is in play.
- May call other domain logics (nested `@atomic` reuses the session); **never** another domain’s repository.

### `services/`
- Thin transport: HTTP/gRPC → domain InputDTO → logic → OutputDTO.
- Version in path: `services/{domain}/v{n}/`. No business rules / `@atomic` here.

### `helpers/` (optional)
- Pure utilities only — no database calls, no network I/O, no adapter construction.
- Sub-packages: `utils/`, `decorators/`, `interceptors/`.
- Prefer ArchiPy helpers before writing custom ones.
- Decorators and interceptors must not import concrete adapter implementations at module level.

### `configs/`
- Extend `archipy.configs.base_config.BaseConfig`.
- Call `BaseConfig.set_global(config)` once at bootstrap.
- No hardcoded secrets — document required vars in `.env.example`.

## Import Direction (one-way)

```text
configs  ←  models  ←  helpers  ←  repositories / logics / services
```

- Never import upward (e.g. models must not import repositories or logics).
- Services/logics may depend on ports (abstractions), not concrete adapter modules when DI is used.

## Python Conventions (apps)

- Python 3.14+: `X | Y`, `list[str]`, `dict[str, int]`.
- Double quotes for strings.
- Google-style docstrings on public functions/classes.
- Specific exceptions; always `raise ... from e`.
